            <ArticleTitle>[Effect of blockaders of cardiac beta-adrenergic structures on the central hemodynamics and contractile function of the myocardium in acute experimental coronary insufficiency].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>100-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The indices of central hemodynamics and myocardial contractile function were studied in 35 dogs before and in different periods after the administration of drugs which block beta-receptors: propranolol, pindolol, and talinolol. The drugs blocking the beta-adrenergic receptors were administered against the background of an intact myocardium to 15 dogs and against the background of acute coronary insufficiency to another 15; acute ischemia was induced in 5 dogs to which the drugs were not given. It was established that beta-adrenergic blocking agents have a beneficial effect in the acute stage of myocardial ischemia; they exert a marked influence on the consumption of oxygen by the myocardium, intramyocardial tension, and the contractile capacity and rhythm of the heart. Talinolol produced the most favourable effect.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
